SeaweedFS + WSL2 + Docker Desktop + AWS CLI Setup Guide for Windows

Install WSL2
Open PowerShell as Administrator and run:
wsl --list --online
wsl --install -d Ubuntu-26.04
If Ubuntu is already installed, confirm WSL version:
wsl -l -v

Install Docker Desktop for Windows
Download and install Docker Desktop from:
https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/
After install, open Docker Desktop and go to Settings > General.
Enable:
"Use the WSL 2 based engine"

Verify Docker is working
Open a fresh PowerShell window and run:
docker --version
docker compose version

Put the SeaweedFS Docker Compose file in a folder
Example folder:
C:\mywork\loghawk\src\loghawk\admin

Use the following file:
docker-compose.yml

version: "3.8"

services:
master:
image: chrislusf/seaweedfs:latest
container_name: seaweed-master
command: "master -ip=master -port=9333"
ports:
- "9333:9333"
- "19333:19333"
networks:
- seaweed

volume:
image: chrislusf/seaweedfs:latest
container_name: seaweed-volume
command: "volume -mserver=master:9333 -dir=/data -port=8080"
depends_on:
- master
volumes:
- C:/seaweedfs/data:/data
ports:
- "8080:8080"
networks:
- seaweed

filer:
image: chrislusf/seaweedfs:latest
container_name: seaweed-filer
command: "filer -master=master:9333 -port=8888"
depends_on:
- master
- volume
ports:
- "8888:8888"
networks:
- seaweed

s3:
image: chrislusf/seaweedfs:latest
container_name: seaweed-s3
command: "s3 -filer=filer:8888 -port=8333"
depends_on:
- filer
ports:
- "8333:8333"
networks:
- seaweed

networks:
seaweed:
driver: bridge

Start SeaweedFS with Docker Compose
From the folder containing docker-compose.yml:
cd C:\mywork\loghawk\src\loghawk\admin
docker compose up -d
Check running containers:
docker ps

Install AWS CLI on Windows
Open PowerShell and run:
winget install Amazon.AWSCLI
Then verify:
aws --version

Configure local S3 credentials
Set dummy credentials because SeaweedFS is S3-compatible but not AWS:
set AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=somekey
set AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=somesecret
set AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

Test SeaweedFS S3 endpoint
List buckets:
aws --endpoint-url http://localhost:8333 s3 ls

Create a bucket
aws --endpoint-url http://localhost:8333 s3 mb s3://mybucket

Upload a sample file
aws --endpoint-url http://localhost:8333 s3 cp loghawk_sample_logs.json s3://mybucket/sample_logs.json

Verify the uploaded file
aws --endpoint-url http://localhost:8333 s3 ls s3://mybucket

Notes

SeaweedFS provides an S3-compatible API, not Azure Blob protocol.
Docker Desktop should be running with WSL2 backend.
The local AWS CLI credentials are dummy values for the local object store.
For better Docker performance, keep your project in the WSL filesystem if possible.
Troubleshooting
If Docker compose is not recognized:
Ensure Docker Desktop is installed and running
Ensure WSL2 backend is enabled
Restart PowerShell or the machine
If AWS CLI says "Unable to locate credentials":
Set the dummy credentials again:
set AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=somekey
set AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=somesecret
set AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

If the SeaweedFS endpoint is not reachable:

Check docker ps
Check docker compose logs
Confirm the S3 port 8333 is exposed
